FreeSDN legacy queue inventory — read-only review, no migration
=============================================================

Scope and meaning of success
----------------------------
scripts/inventory_legacy_queues.py inventories the in-memory dataset of ONE
isolated disposable Redis/Valkey copy. Run it separately for each retained source
that needs comparison. It does not choose an elected primary, load/validate a
backup, merge sources, copy queues, restore jobs, change HA markers, or authorize
replay. An exit status of 0 means the supported inventory was complete during its
observation window. It does NOT mean a source is authoritative, its backup was
fully loaded, or any hardware operation is safe to repeat. Even an empty receipt
is not proof that the original deployment was drained.

The old Sentinel URL database suffix defect can leave broker/result data in
DB 0, 1 or 2. See HA-UPGRADING. This tool scans every configured database (up to
64), understands FreeSDN's five standard queues and priority lists in DB 0/1/2,
and rejects nonempty extra databases. Keep the original volumes, anonymous
replica volume, Sentinel configurations, exact images and application backups.
Record the observed election, roles, replication IDs/offsets and writer-fencing
procedure privately before stopping the old deployment. The clone's current
replication metadata is not a substitute for that original evidence.

Preparing a source (operator-controlled; not an automated restore)
-----------------------------------------------------------------
Use a SECOND disposable copy of retained backups, never the sole backup or an
installation. Verify the copy was fully loaded using the retained server version,
source counts/fingerprints and the actual persistence format. AOF-only data will
NOT load if you simply start a server with appendonly=no. Where AOF replay requires
writes, use a separate writable scratch copy, validate the resulting dataset,
then disable persistence in that disposable server before inventory. This tool
does not qualify that conversion or a backup format/version upgrade.

The running source container must satisfy ALL of these guards:

- --network=none; no published ports, extra networks or namespace peers.
- --read-only; no writable persistent mounts. A disposable /data tmpfs is allowed.
  Persisted backup/config mounts must remain read-only. A read-only Docker socket
  mount is not safe: never mount a Docker/host control socket into a source copy.
- No privileged mode, host PID/IPC, added capabilities/devices, restart policy or
  healthcheck. Use --no-healthcheck to override an inherited image healthcheck.
- Label freesdn.queue-inventory.disposable-copy=true. This is an operator assertion
  of copy provenance; a label cannot prove that a backup is complete or disposable.
- Valkey listening on 127.0.0.1:6379, cluster mode off, appendonly=no and save="".
  The probe must be its only connected client. No shell/exec writer, worker,
  producer, Sentinel, scheduler or other administrator may access it during the
  read. Do not bypass the deployed HA entrypoint's old-volume/identity guards.

The tool uses one non-root, read-only, capability-free helper container in the
source's isolated network namespace, with bounded memory/process count and no
mounts/ports. It resolves the retained trusted backend image to its immutable ID;
only Python and redis-py from that image are used, not the FreeSDN application or
Celery consumers. It removes only its own labelled helper. It never stops or
removes the source. Docker access is required; a malicious Docker administrator
can defeat isolation, so do not run concurrent administrative changes.

Running the inventory
---------------------
Use a private POSIX filesystem, not a Windows mount without POSIX permissions.
Create an owner-only (0600), regular, non-symlink auth JSON file, for example:

    {"username": "default", "password": "REPLACE_WITH_PRIVATE_COPY_PASSWORD"}

Do not put credentials in shell arguments, a Redis URL, logs or a release archive.
Omit --auth-file only if the isolated copy deliberately has no authentication.
Use a NEW absolute directory for each run; it is created with permissions 0700.

    python3 scripts/inventory_legacy_queues.py \
      --source-container YOUR_DISPOSABLE_COPY \
      --backend-image YOUR_RETAINED_TRUSTED_BACKEND_IMAGE \
      --auth-file /private/path/copy-auth.json \
      --work-dir /private/path/new-queue-inventory

Add --docker /path/to/docker if needed. The host script needs only the Python
standard library. The retained image supplies redis-py. The source must support
PEXPIRETIME (Redis 7+/Valkey); unsupported commands fail closed.

Only receipt.json is intended to be shared. source.json, probe-output.json,
cleanup.json and failure logs are PRIVATE: they can contain infrastructure
identifiers, task IDs/names, routing/binding metadata or confidential diagnostics.
The manifest does not include task arguments, result bodies or credentials, but
identifiers themselves can be sensitive. Never publish the entire work directory.
The tool writes an initial incomplete receipt before Docker work, and atomically
replaces it on completion. Host power loss/disk failure can still interrupt writes.
If probe_removed=false, inspect private cleanup.json, verify the recorded helper's
ID and ownership label, then remove that helper only. Keep the source and backups.

What is examined
----------------
Two complete scans compare key types, opaque DUMP hashes and absolute expirations.
Server identity/role/replication offsets, write/expiry/eviction/connection counters,
and command accounting must agree. The check detects an intervening write/delete
although the final bytes match. It describes that observation window, not an
ongoing lock. Expiring keys can make a copy change naturally; retry on a properly
prepared disposable snapshot and retain any incomplete receipt.

For standard Kombu protocol-2 JSON envelopes, the private manifest records task
ID/name, opaque body/envelope/execution hashes, routing and priority. Task bodies
are never unpickled, executed, or decoded into task arguments. Queued list_index
is the Redis left-to-right index; fifo_index=0 is the next item from RPOP for that
physical list. There is no promised total execution order across separate queues.
Reserved unacked envelopes and unacked_index delivery timestamps are reconciled;
missing mates are errors. Binding sets and JSON result status/expiry/hash are
recorded without result payloads. SUCCESS/FAILURE/REVOKED are completion evidence,
not proof of a safe replay decision. Missing or expired results mean UNKNOWN.

Duplicate IDs, IDs in several databases, conflicting execution payloads/result
metadata, queued/reserved overlap and completion evidence appear in private
reconciliation and sanitized aggregate counts. All work stays quarantined for
operator/application/device review. Nothing is automatically classified as safe
for replay, even when there are no duplicates.

Unsupported keys (including cache/heartbeat/control keys), prefixes, custom
queues/routing/bindings, priority schemes, protocol-1/non-JSON bodies, malformed
records and nonempty DBs beyond 2 produce an INCOMPLETE inventory. This deliberately
conservative first version cannot certify a mixed application cache as fully
understood. Do not delete unknown records to force a green result; preserve the
copy and review/extend the format handling before a migration is supported.

Bounds and remaining acceptance
-------------------------------
Defaults: 10,000 keys, 10,000 records (messages/index entries/bindings/results),
4 MiB per value, 64 MiB examined per scan, 180-second helper timeout. CLI limits
have hard caps. An oversized server reply may exhaust the 512 MiB helper before a
Python limit is checked; the host then retains an incomplete receipt. No safe or
empty conclusion follows from timeout, authentication failure or a truncated run.

scripts/check_queue_inventory.py exercises real Valkey and Celery/Kombu data with
synthetic secrets in disposable containers. scripts/tests/test_queue_inventory.py
covers unsafe-source guards, credential-file restrictions and engine failures.
These checks do not qualify target HA bootstrap, queue restoration/actual worker
consumption after cutover, password rotation, rollback after target/device writes,
production-scale RTO/RPO, or real hardware acceptance. Those remain release gates.

